00:07:45.841 --> 00:07:46.674
Still.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:47.790
And especially like,
technology has disrupted

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.430
so many industries.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.560
Every industry, healthcare,
how we buy stuff.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.531
I mean, everything.

00:07:53.531 --> 00:07:55.261
But yet it hasn't quite disrupted

00:07:55.261 --> 00:07:57.290
how we teach in the classroom.

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:59.022
It should have. It should.

00:07:59.022 --> 00:08:00.331
- Which is interesting,
'cause you thought COVID

00:08:00.331 --> 00:08:01.440
would've changed that a tiny bit,

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.789
just 'cause we all were
on Zoom, but anyway.

00:08:02.789 --> 00:08:04.521
Glad that's not still happening.

00:08:04.521 --> 00:08:05.601
So, let's talk about
your new movie though,

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:06.462
"Flamin' Hot."

00:08:06.462 --> 00:08:08.990
What made you wanna direct this?

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:11.171
- Well, the story made its way to me,

00:08:11.171 --> 00:08:12.901
it's a story about Richard Montanez,

00:08:12.901 --> 00:08:14.371
who was a janitor who worked at Frito-Lay

00:08:14.371 --> 00:08:17.454
and had a huge hand in helping launch

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:19.862
the number one snack in the world,

00:08:19.862 --> 00:08:21.669
which is Flaming Hot Cheetos.

00:08:21.669 --> 00:08:25.662
And it's such a beautiful, inspirational,

00:08:25.662 --> 00:08:27.462
motivational story.

00:08:27.462 --> 00:08:30.691
Like this guy had the hardest upbringing

00:08:30.691 --> 00:08:31.550
and the hardest life,

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:33.089
he couldn't even read or write,

00:08:33.089 --> 00:08:34.610
didn't really finish high school.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:35.999
I mean, nothing.

00:08:35.999 --> 00:08:38.069
And is super successful today,

00:08:38.069 --> 00:08:40.708
because he had this drive
and ambition and discipline

00:08:40.708 --> 00:08:43.490
and he had this insane belief in himself.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:45.081
And so when I read his
story and I was like,

00:08:45.081 --> 00:08:46.441
"This happened?"

00:08:46.441 --> 00:08:50.194
And it's one of my favorite
snacks in the world.

00:08:50.194 --> 00:08:51.939
- Says literally every human I know.

00:08:51.939 --> 00:08:54.401
Everybody loves Flaming Hot, yeah.

00:08:54.401 --> 00:08:55.299
- Everybody loves Flaming Hot.

00:08:55.299 --> 00:08:56.989
The first time I ate a
regular bag of Cheetos,

00:08:56.989 --> 00:08:58.419
I was like, "Oh, I got a bad bag."

00:08:58.419 --> 00:09:00.240
- You were like disappointed.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.270
- Yeah. I didn't know
there was regular Cheetos.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:03.529
- They forgot the flavoring in this.

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:05.710
- They forgot the flavoring in this bag.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.670
And they were like, "No,
that's how Cheetos taste.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:08.978
You're eating the hot ones."

00:09:08.978 --> 00:09:11.030
And I was like, "What?
There's normal ones?"

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:11.863
- It's wrong and rude.

00:09:11.863 --> 00:09:12.990
- Why do they sell the normal ones?

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.201
- I love the heart of that story

00:09:15.201 --> 00:09:17.950
and I love that like how
many people will connect to

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.378
this character of like
maybe you don't achieve

00:09:20.378 --> 00:09:23.361
what society tells you you need
to achieve to be successful,

00:09:23.361 --> 00:09:25.990
but you still have it
in you to make waves.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:27.990
- Especially for women,
when women are told like,

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.029
"Well, that job's not for
you," or a person of color,

00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:31.862
like, "Oh, you know,

00:09:31.862 --> 00:09:34.048
opportunities aren't for people like you."

00:09:34.048 --> 00:09:36.048
- Yeah, love to hear it.

00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:39.427
- He was like, he dared to
ask why not, why not me?

00:09:39.427 --> 00:09:41.358
"Why can't I come up with something great?

00:09:41.358 --> 00:09:43.847
I have a great idea.
Why can't I be heard?"

00:09:43.847 --> 00:09:46.436
And I think a lot of
people are gonna identify

00:09:46.436 --> 00:09:48.689
with that journey and that adversity.

00:09:48.689 --> 00:09:50.689
We've all bumped up against it.

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:52.499
- I know that you've directed for years.

00:09:52.499 --> 00:09:54.009
But is this your first like movie?

00:09:54.009 --> 00:09:54.867
- It's my first movie.

00:09:54.867 --> 00:09:55.700
- Okay, I was like,

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:56.558
'cause you've been doing
it for like a decade.

00:09:56.558 --> 00:09:57.636
For shows, right?

00:09:57.636 --> 00:09:59.467
- 10 years in TV, yeah.

00:09:59.467 --> 00:10:01.129
- That's awesome to make that that jump,

00:10:01.129 --> 00:10:03.478
'cause it's a lot, I love this.

00:10:03.478 --> 00:10:04.367
- We ate a lot of Cheetos.

00:10:04.367 --> 00:10:06.398
- You do like food. (laughs)

00:10:06.398 --> 00:10:09.630
So, in the film, the main
character has a side hustle

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.277
as a kid, selling burritos.

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:12.619
So, did you have a side hustle as a kid?

00:10:12.619 --> 00:10:14.718
- Oh, yeah. I had a lot of side hustles.

00:10:14.718 --> 00:10:15.887
I had so many side hustles.

00:10:15.887 --> 00:10:17.609
The main side hustle, I was like eight

00:10:17.609 --> 00:10:19.526
and I would mow lawns.

00:10:20.956 --> 00:10:21.789
Did you mow lawns?

00:10:21.789 --> 00:10:22.622
- Yes, I mowed lawns.

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:25.009
- My dad was like, "I'll give
you $20 to mow the lawn."

00:10:25.009 --> 00:10:26.689
So I did that like Sunday and I go,

00:10:26.689 --> 00:10:29.019
"Okay, so if I mow it Monday,
Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday,

00:10:29.019 --> 00:10:30.259
I'll have a hundred dollars on Friday,

00:10:30.259 --> 00:10:31.579
my dad's like, "Not my lawn.

00:10:31.579 --> 00:10:32.910
You're not gonna mow our lawn."

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:33.743
- It has to grow back.

00:10:33.743 --> 00:10:35.009
- It has to grow.

00:10:35.009 --> 00:10:37.390
And I was like, "Oh, I
just wanted to mow ours."

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:38.718
So then I would ask all the neighbors

00:10:38.718 --> 00:10:40.609
if I could mow their lawns.

00:10:40.609 --> 00:10:41.649
- That's smart.

00:10:41.649 --> 00:10:43.990
I think it's so awesome when kids,

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:45.249
my kids are six and eight

00:10:45.249 --> 00:10:46.710
and they still are like,
"How can I earn money?"

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:47.837
And I'm like, "Okay."

00:10:47.837 --> 00:10:49.339
I like instilling that.

00:10:49.339 --> 00:10:51.237
And even that, I make
them like, they're like,

00:10:51.237 --> 00:10:52.070
"I want a toy."

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.289
And I'm like, "Cool, I've given you a lot,

00:10:53.289 --> 00:10:54.447
so you can buy your own,

00:10:54.447 --> 00:10:55.918
if you wanna go to Target right now."

00:10:55.918 --> 00:10:57.209
And they'll use their own money.

00:10:57.209 --> 00:10:59.138
And then they're like, "Wait, so wait,

00:10:59.138 --> 00:10:59.971
I don't want that toy."

00:10:59.971 --> 00:11:01.910
It just teaches them like money
management at a young age.

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:03.649
- Yeah, they said one of
the greatest indicators of

00:11:03.649 --> 00:11:07.550
a successful adult is
not Ivy League schools

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:08.499
or if they went to Harvard,

00:11:08.499 --> 00:11:11.529
it's did they have chores
when they were a kid?

00:11:11.529 --> 00:11:13.698
Did they understand I have to do this,

00:11:13.698 --> 00:11:17.230
and the responsibility of
doing something, and so...

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:18.397
- Wow, my mom did really well then,

00:11:18.397 --> 00:11:19.318
'cause I had-

00:11:19.318 --> 00:11:20.937
- I had so many chores.

00:11:20.937 --> 00:11:21.770
Oh my God.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:24.179
- I was the only kid in the
house, so I had all of them.

00:11:24.179 --> 00:11:26.219
- Oh, you did?

00:11:26.219 --> 00:11:27.509
I had a lot of chores too.

00:11:27.509 --> 00:11:28.579
- That's good, though.

00:11:28.579 --> 00:11:30.249
It teaches you responsibility.

00:11:30.249 --> 00:11:31.498
And I always tell my kids too, I'm like,

00:11:31.498 --> 00:11:33.009
"You are getting paid.

00:11:33.009 --> 00:11:34.939
I'm paying for the roof of your head."

00:11:34.939 --> 00:11:36.310
- Oh, God. You became your mom!

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:37.158
- I am.

00:11:37.158 --> 00:11:39.236
I'm okay with it, yeah.

00:11:39.236 --> 00:11:42.169
I came out all right,
so this should be fine.

00:11:42.169 --> 00:11:44.969
Well, it's debatable, but what do you hope

00:11:44.969 --> 00:11:47.278
people take away from the
movie, like the message?

00:11:47.278 --> 00:11:48.278
- You know, I hope they leave,

00:11:48.278 --> 00:11:51.739
I love, everybody who's
seen the film feels so good

00:11:51.739 --> 00:11:52.572
when they leave.

00:11:52.572 --> 00:11:53.619
You feel so good.

00:11:53.619 --> 00:11:55.571
And I feel like we don't
have those movies anymore.

00:11:55.571 --> 00:11:58.081
Like I love all the Marvel
and all that stuff too,

00:11:58.081 --> 00:11:58.914
but like-

00:11:58.914 --> 00:12:00.550
- I know, and I like
the big epic dramas too,

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.139
but I don't wanna be
like super sad either.

00:12:02.139 --> 00:12:03.029
I wanna feel good.

00:12:03.029 --> 00:12:04.731
- I wanna feel good when I watch a movie

00:12:04.731 --> 00:12:05.841
and I wanna learn something.

00:12:05.841 --> 00:12:09.559
And I think people leave are
gonna leave watching this film

00:12:09.559 --> 00:12:11.291
with like, "What can I do?"

00:12:11.291 --> 00:12:12.777
- That's, I think that people
are gonna leave this film,

00:12:12.777 --> 00:12:13.891
going dreaming.

00:12:13.891 --> 00:12:14.724
- [Eva] Dreaming.

00:12:14.724 --> 00:12:16.081
- Yeah, and I think that's really cool.

00:12:16.081 --> 00:12:19.691
- And that's, you know, a really big task.

00:12:19.691 --> 00:12:22.370
And I think we accomplished it, yeah.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:23.203
- [Kelly] Keep dreaming.

00:12:23.203 --> 00:12:24.036
- Keep dreaming.

00:12:24.036 --> 00:12:25.113
- There's no age limit there.

